window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-12-2", "source": "2023年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the university newspaper is inviting submissions from the students for its coming edition on the recent development in their hometown.You are now to write an essay for submission. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Over the past few years, my hometown has undergone remarkable changes. As a student writing for our\nuniversity newspaper, I would like to share two of them that have impressed me the most: transportation and\nthe environment.\nIn the past, it used to take us hours of bumpy driving to reach the provincial capital. Now, however, a new\nhigh-speed railway gets us there in just two hours, and the widened roads have made daily travel far more\nconvenient. Another change worth mentioning is the environment. The small river that once smelled terrible\nhas been cleaned up, and several green parks have been built along its banks, where families now enjoy\nwalking after dinner.\nAll these changes have made my hometown a much better place to live in. As someone who grew up there, I\nfeel truly proud, and I firmly believe that it will become even more beautiful and livable in the years to come."}, "translation": {"prompt": "改革开放以来，中国人的饮食(diet)发生了显著变化。过去由于经济落后，食品种类有限、数量不足 ，人们仅仅满足于吃得饱。如今中国经济快速发展，食品不仅更加丰富多样，质量也大幅提高。随着生活水平不断提升，人们对饮食的要求越来越高，更加注重吃得营养健康。因此，目前市场上推出的低脂、低糖、有机食品受到人们的普遍欢迎。", "sample": "Since the reform and opening-up, the diet of Chinese people has undergone significant changes. In the past, due to the backward economy, food was limited in variety and insufficient in quantity, so people were content with merely having enough to eat. Today, with China's economy developing rapidly, food is not only more abundant and diverse but also greatly improved in quality. As living standards keep rising, people have increasingly higher demands for their diet and pay more attention to eating in a nutritious and healthy way. Therefore, the low-fat, low-sugar and organic food launched on the market today is widely welcomed by people."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2023-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "A number of studies have looked at how family life can affect productivity and satisfaction in the workplace. However, there has been  __26__  little research on the influence of leisure activities. So Ciara Kelly and colleagues recruited 129 hobbyists to look at how the time spent on their hobbies  __27__  their work life.\nThe researchers found that when participants spent longer than  __28__  on their leisure activity, their belief in their ability to perform their job was strengthened. But this was only the  __29__  when they had a serious hobby that was dissimilar to their job, or when their hobby was similar to their work but they only did it  __30__  . When their hobby was both serious and similar to their job, then spending more time on it actually decreased their work  __31__  .\nWhy might that be? To maintain a serious hobby, people need to invest significant psychological resources, say the authors—so if the activity has the same kinds of demands as their work, they may be left  __32__  and unable to perform well at their job. But if their hobby is quite different from their career, it may not  __33__  in the same way but instead help them develop other knowledge and skills that can  __34__  their confidence at work. \"Consider a scientist who is a keen rock climber,\" says Kelly. \"Since climbing is so far  __35__  from their day-to-day work activities, they can still recover from the demands of their job with plenty of resources.\"", "matchIntro": "More fathers are taking paternity leave, but mothers are still doing all the work", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Attitudes towards paternity leave(陪产假) have drastically changed in America in the last five years as more fathers feel comfortable taking extended time off, but gender bias persists when it comes to career prospects and the home, according to a new study of working parents."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Research by the Boston College Center for Work & Family, which surveyed new parents at four large US companies who were qualified for taking at least six weeks paid parental leave, found that 81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed said the notion of fathers taking leave has become more acceptable."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Of those surveyed, 62% of fathers took the maximum amount of time off compared to 93% of mothers, and around three-quarters of workers said their employer was equally supportive of mothers and fathers taking parental leave and over half said leave policies had made workplace culture better."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The US is one of only three countries in the world not to offer statutory(法定的) paid leave, but increasingly states and companies are starting to take up the issue. So far, eight states and the District of Columbia have their own paid family leave laws."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Brad Harrington, executive director of the center and lead author of the study, estimates only 20% to 30% of companies in the US offer paid parental leave. He feels the research findings reflect an obvious change in corporate attitudes to new fathers taking time off."}, {"label": "F", "text": "\"We did a study on paternity leave five years ago. Compared with those findings, these numbers were shocking to me. I did not expect 80% of people to say the organisation finds dads taking this leave acceptable and three-quarters to say it's equally supportive of women and men taking leave,\" Harrington said."}, {"label": "G", "text": "The previous study found that nearly three-quarters of fathers saw two to four weeks as an appropriate duration for paternity leave and 76% said they would prefer not to take all their time off at once."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Since then, there have been a number of legal cases against companies involving paternity leave—including cases against JPMorgan Chase and Estee Lauder—which have helped put pressure on companies to make their parental leave policies gender neutral."}, {"label": "I", "text": "However, the study also shows how traditional gender roles endure both at work, where more women than men reported changes in their perceived career potential, and at home—even among workers who claim to have a strong desire for equality."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The vast majority of men, 97%, said one of the top reasons to take leave was to share caregiving with their partner. But when they were asked about how caregiving and household tasks were divided, their answers painted a different picture. While about 75% of employees said both genders should give the same amount of care, the majority of men and nearly half of women admitted that in reality the female actually did most of it. A tiny fraction, 2%, of men said they did more of the childcare."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Men and women's experiences of the return to work following parental leave were also considerably different. Of the women surveyed, 32% reported a downturn in their job satisfaction, while 14% said it increased. In comparison, 17% of men said their job satisfaction went down and 20% said it went up. Meanwhile, more women reported an increase in their responsibilities and manager expectations after childbirth. Half of the women said they used flexible work arrangements after becoming a parent, while just 27% of men did. Similar percentages of men and women said they enjoyed their careers and that it gave them a sense of achievement, while around half of women and 44% of men said it was a key part of their identity."}, {"label": "L", "text": "On the subject of career advancement, 59% of women and 49% of men said leave could be limiting and both genders said they feared it would have an impact on their progress long-term. But on opportunity for promotion, more than double the number of women, 30% compared with 15% of men, believed their chances to be lower after becoming a parent. Despite progress, the struggle for women to reach the highest positions of power is demonstrated in this year's Fortune 500 list, which featured a record 33 female CEOs, but this still represents a tiny fraction of the total."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Harrington said culture change depends on companies putting more focus on men and their responsibilities. \"By that I mean companies need to give men paternity leave and encourage men to take time off to be with their kids early on in the kids' life. They also need to recognise that men have to make significant adjustments when they become parents. Companies cannot do all these things to enhance women's advancement and then turn around and say, 'Oh, but we don't expect the men to take over for the women at home.'\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "In May, the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) and Outten & Golden LLP announced a historic class-action $5m settlement with JPMorgan Chase on behalf of male employees who claim they were illegally denied access to paid parental leave. Derek Rotondo, 35, filed the discrimination charge against his company after he was allegedly told by his HR department that mothers were considered primary caregivers. Thus, they were allowed to take 16 weeks of paid parental leave. Fathers, however, could take just two weeks."}, {"label": "O", "text": "The father of two from Columbus, Ohio, who still works at the company as an associate and investigator, said he has witnessed a \"domino effect(多米诺效应)\" across companies since the settlement, but that there is still substantial progress to be made towards changing attitudes towards paternity leave."}, {"label": "P", "text": "\"I do think there's still some way to go…there's still going to be sort of the unstated expectation for new dads to essentially come right back to work, but I think the research is showing that's starting to change.\" He said equal parental leave is an essential component to creating gender equality in the workplace. \"The old standard of women staying home, having babies and cooking doesn't apply and hasn't applied for a long time.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.\nFinding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a \"win-at-all-costs\" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success. One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.\nOne reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\nThe sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.", "47": "Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.\nFinding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a \"win-at-all-costs\" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success. One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.\nOne reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\nThe sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.", "48": "Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.\nFinding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a \"win-at-all-costs\" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success. One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.\nOne reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\nThe sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.", "49": "Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.\nFinding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a \"win-at-all-costs\" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success. One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.\nOne reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\nThe sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.", "50": "Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your overall performance. But before you go out and find an entrepreneur to outcompete, it's important to understand and avoid the traps that often come with rivalry. After all, competitive rivalry can also hinder effective decision-making and increase your willingness to take risks, behaviors that can ultimately hurt your venture's success.\nFinding someone you're committed to outcompeting can be a great way to stay focused on your goals and push your venture to the next level. But when you're intently focused on outperforming your rivals, you may begin to develop a \"win-at-all-costs\" mentality that causes you to ignore how you achieve success. One group of researchers, for example, examined the link between rivalry and unethical behavior. They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave unethically to win. But such behavior may stain your reputation and strain relationships important to your success. One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important. While outperforming your rivals may provide short-term benefits, the loss of your integrity will have long-term consequences.\nOne reason having a rival can enhance your venture's performance is that it creates a level of excitement that drives you to work harder. But this eagerness to win may also hurt your venture's success, particularly when it causes you to make impulsive, insensible decisions. But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical thinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses, especially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\nThe sense of eagerness that comes with having a rival can not only cause you to make poorer decisions, but it can also lead you to take greater risks that put your venture in peril. One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your venture's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such feelings are affecting your decision-making.", "51": "A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which—apart from the obvious differences—is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time. However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker?\nFor most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers. What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.\nHowever, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.\nThe business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.\nThe benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.", "52": "A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which—apart from the obvious differences—is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time. However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker?\nFor most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers. What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.\nHowever, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.\nThe business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.\nThe benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.", "53": "A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which—apart from the obvious differences—is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time. However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker?\nFor most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers. What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.\nHowever, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.\nThe business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.\nThe benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.", "54": "A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which—apart from the obvious differences—is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time. However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker?\nFor most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers. What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.\nHowever, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.\nThe business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.\nThe benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.", "55": "A multitasker is one who can perform two or more tasks effectively at the same time, which—apart from the obvious differences—is similar to what a computer does. The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time. However, the question is: can a person really be a multitasker?\nFor most scientists, the answer is no. So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not handle multitasking situations well. As soon as two tasks require our attention, productivity suffers. What we call multitasking, therefore, is in reality the ability to move more or less quickly from one task to another. This requires two essential conditions: that one of the tasks needs to be automatic, like walking or eating, and that they both need different mental processes. Answering the phone and writing at the same time, for example.\nHowever, on the other side of the coin there are people who maintain that it is possible to be, or at least seem to be, multitasking. A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks or not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on their performance.\nThe business perspective offers a different view: multitasking is understood as the ability to adapt to all types of environment within a company and effectively undertake different activities within a set time frame. Indeed, many companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking to improve their productivity. From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be taught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their working practices.\nThe benefits of multitasking are clear. Being quicker and more efficient increases our performance and the number of tasks completed. But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are reduced and that can lead to more mistakes."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "Why did the police officer stop the SUV car?", "options": ["A) He noticed the driver was too young to drive.", "B) He found there was no one sitting at the wheel.", "C) He thought something must be wrong with the driver.", "D) He saw the driver changing lanes much too frequently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He thought something must be wrong with the driver.\n【定位】A police officer in the U.S. stopped a large SUV car that was going very slowly and drifting\nacross lanes on a highway. He expected to find a driver who was either very drunk or having\na medical emergency.\n美国一名警察拦下了一辆在高速公路上行驶得非常缓慢、还在车道间飘忽乱晃的大型 SUV。\n他原以为会发现一个要么烂醉、要么突发疾病的司机。\n【信号】开篇即点题、紧跟一句\"原以为\"的推断。新闻把核心事件放在第一句：车 going very\nslowly and drifting across lanes（开得很慢、车道间乱飘）。紧接着 He expected to\nfind a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency 这句\"警察原\n以为\"，直接道出他判断\"司机一定出了问题\"——这正是拦车的真实理由。\n【替换】a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency ↔\nsomething must be wrong with the driver\n具体病况概括成\"司机有问题\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音后面确实揭晓司机是 a five-year-old boy（五岁男孩），A 项 He noticed the driver\nwas too young to drive.（发现司机太小不能开车）就盗用了这个\"年龄小\"的事实。但那是\n警察拦车\"之后\"才发现的真相，并非拦车的原因——拦车时他根本看不清开车的是谁。听\n到\"小孩\"就选 A，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a five-year-old boy sat on the edge of the driver's seat\nB) 与原文相反\nB 项 He found there was no one sitting at the wheel.（发现没人坐在方向盘后）听起来\n很像那个离奇画面，但录音说的是男孩 sat on the edge of the driver's seat——其实有人\n坐在驾驶位，只是个小孩。而且\"座位上情况\"是拦车后才看清的，拦车当下警察只看到车乱\n晃。B 既与原文不符、又答非所问。\n借用原文：a five-year-old boy sat on the edge of the driver's seat\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 He saw the driver changing lanes much too frequently.（看到司机频繁变道）盗\n用了原文 drifting across lanes 里的 lanes。但 drifting across lanes 是\"车失控地在车道\n间飘移乱晃\"（因为开车的是够不着踏板的小孩），不是司机主动、频繁地变换车道。D\n把\"失控飘移\"偷换成\"频繁变道\"，看似沾边实则两回事。\n借用原文：drifting across lanes on a highway", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the boy tell the police officers he was planning to do?", "options": ["A) Buy a sports car.", "B) Drive across town.", "C) Leave California.", "D) Visit his sister."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Buy a sports car.\n【定位】The boy later told confused officers that he was planning to travel to California and buy a\nLamborghini sports car.\n男孩后来告诉一脸困惑的警察，他打算开去加利福尼亚，买一辆兰博基尼跑车。\n【信号】planning to 直接引出打算做的事。信号词 he was planning to travel to California and\nbuy a Lamborghini sports car 里的 planning to 正是\"表打算\"的标志，其后跟着的两个动\n作（去加州、买跑车）就是男孩的计划，是题目要的答案落点。\n【替换】buy a Lamborghini sports car ↔ Buy a sports car.\n去掉品牌名后即同一动作\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Drive across town.（开车穿过城镇）盗用了原文 drove three kilometers across\ntown 里的 across town。但\"开车穿城\"是男孩已经做过的事（他已经开了三公里穿过城镇\n上高速），不是他\"打算\"做的事。题目问的是 planning to 之后的计划，B 把\"已发生\"偷换\n成\"打算做\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：He then drove three kilometers across town before getting on the highway.\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项 Leave California.（离开加州）盗用了地名 California，但录音说的是 travel to\nCalifornia（去往加州），方向恰好相反——是去、不是离开。地名一念到极易被耳朵抓\n住，但方向词（to / leave）被悄悄反转，最易让人误选。\n借用原文：he was planning to travel to California\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Visit his sister.（看望他姐姐）盗用了原文出现的 sister。但 sister 在录音里只是\"趁姐\n姐午睡时拿了车钥匙\"的背景人物（while his teenage sister was napping），男孩的计划\n是去加州买跑车，从没说过要去看望姐姐。这是把一个出现过的人名硬塞进\"计划\"里。\n借用原文：while his teenage sister was napping\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Mobile phones have changed the way we live, how we read, work, communicate, and shop. But\nwe already know this. What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us\nare changing our skeletons, possibly altering not just the way we behave but even the very shape of\nour bodies.\n手机已经改变了我们的生活方式，改变了我们阅读、工作、交流和购物的方式。但我们已经知道这一点。\n我们尚未理解的是，我们面前的这些小型机器正在改变我们的骨骼，可能不仅改变我们的行为方式，甚至\n改变我们身体的形状。\n2 New scientific research at the University of the Sunshine Coast in Queensland, Australia, suggests that\nyoung people are developing extra pieces of bone at the backs of their heads. These pieces of bone are\ncaused by the way people bend their heads when they use the phone. This shifts weight onto the\nmuscles at the back of the head and causes the bone to grow in a way that is not normal. This process\ncan be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing or pressure. The result is a\npiece of bone like a horn that sticks out from the head by the neck.\n澳大利亚昆士兰阳光海岸大学的最新科学研究表明，年轻人的后脑勺正在长出额外的骨块。这些骨块是由\n人们使用手机时低头的方式造成的。这会将重量转移到后脑勺的肌肉上，并导致骨骼以不正常的方式生\n长。这一过程可以与皮肤因持续摩擦或压力而变硬的方式相比较。其结果是在颈部附近从头部突出的一块\n类似角的骨头。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the report say we have not yet understood about mobile phones?", "options": ["A) How they change the way we shop.", "B) How they alter human skeletons.", "C) How they cause increased headaches.", "D) How they affect our communication."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） How they alter human skeletons.\n【定位】What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our\nskeletons, possibly altering not just the way we behave but even the very shape of our bodies.\n我们还没有理解的，是眼前这些小小的机器正在改变我们的骨骼这件事——它们或许不仅改\n变我们的行为方式，甚至会改变我们身体的形状。\n【信号】题干原话 we have not yet understood 直接复现。录音里 What we have not yet\nunderstood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our skeletons 把\n题干的核心短语 \"we have not yet understood\" 原样念出，这种 \"题干关键词在原文出现\"\n就是答案句的定位信号，紧跟其后的 changing our skeletons 即所求。\n【替换】changing our skeletons ↔ How they alter human skeletons.\nchanging 同义替换为 alter\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头 how we read, work, communicate, and shop 确实念到 shop 购物，但紧接着\n说 But we already know this——购物属于 \"我们已经知道\" 的部分，而题目问的是 \"还没\n理解\" 的，正好相反，选它就掉进了原词陷阱。\n借用原文：how we read, work, communicate, and shop\nC) 未提及\n选项 How they cause increased headaches. 说手机导致头痛增多，但整段录音从未提到\nheadaches 头痛，是凭 \"手机看多了会头疼\" 的生活常识硬加的，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 how we read, work, communicate, and shop 念到 communicate 沟通，How\nthey affect our communication. 借此设套；但沟通和购物一样属于 But we already\nknow this 已知的部分，不是题问的 \"还没理解\" 的，故错。\n借用原文：how we read, work, communicate, and shop", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What happens to the skin when rubbed or pressed constantly?", "options": ["A) It loosens.", "B) It brightens.", "C) It hardens.", "D) It softens."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It hardens.\n【定位】This process can be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing\nor pressure.\n这个过程可以类比皮肤在持续摩擦或受压之下变硬的方式。\n【信号】题干关键词 skin、rubbing、pressure 同句聚齐。录音 the skin hardens in response to\nconstant rubbing or pressure 把题干的 skin、rubbed / pressed（原文作 rubbing /\npressure）几个词凑在同一句，关键词集中出现即答案句信号，紧跟的动词 hardens 就是\n结果。\n【替换】the skin hardens ↔ It hardens.\nthe skin 由 It 指代、hardens 原词承接\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt loosens. 说变松，但录音讲皮肤是 hardens 变硬，没有任何 \"变松\" 的说法，纯属无中生\n有。\nB) 未提及\nIt brightens. 说变亮，录音谈的是皮肤受摩擦后 hardens 变硬的物理硬化，与 \"变亮\" 毫不\n相干，录音也未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt softens. 变软，恰与录音 the skin hardens 变硬相反。这是预读时就标好的反义诱选\n——一字方向之差，听不清硬 / 软就会反选。\n借用原文：the skin hardens", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the report say the village is going to do for the cat Wilbur?", "options": ["A) Create Internet pages for him.", "B) Ask a local pet shop to adopt him.", "C) Name an orange tree after him.", "D) Hold a birthday party for him."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Hold a birthday party for him.\n【定位】A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur, who has become\nfamous for making a regular appearance at local shops.\n一座村庄即将为一只名叫 Wilbur 的橘猫举办生日派对，这只猫因为经常出现在当地店铺而\n声名鹊起。\n【信号】开篇 going to 句式即新闻核心事件。新闻第一句 A village is going to throw a birthday\nparty for an orange cat named Wilbur 用 going to 直接给出整条新闻的核心事件，符合\n短篇新闻「开门见山报导何事」的体例，是本题的答案句无疑。\n【替换】is going to throw a birthday party ↔ Hold a birthday party for him.\nthrow=hold 的同义词替换\nfor an orange cat named Wilbur ↔ for him\n具体名词到人称代词的概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Create Internet pages for him. 借用了第二段 Wilbur has his own Internet pages\n这一原词，但原文说的是 Wilbur 已经拥有自己的网页（既成事实），而非村庄「将要为他\n创建」网页，且这件事不是「村庄为他做的事」，属于答非所问加时态错位的双重陷阱。\n借用原文：Wilbur has his own Internet pages\nB) 未提及\n选项 Ask a local pet shop to adopt him. 抓住了录音里反复出现的 local shops 这一概\n念，但录音从未提及 pet shop 或 adopt——Wilbur 是已经有人照看的明星猫，「让宠物\n店领养他」是凭借 cat/shops 自行联想的过度推断。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Name an orange tree after him. 同时盗用了 an orange cat 中的 orange 和\nnamed Wilbur 中的 name，制造「以橘猫之名命名橘树」的错觉，但原文里 orange 修饰\n的是 cat、name 是给猫命名，与「橘树」毫无关系。\n借用原文：an orange cat named Wilbur", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn from the report about the cat?", "options": ["A) He is a bold and aggressive pet.", "B) He pays regular visits to village shops.", "C) He once bit a doctor's receptionist.", "D) He likes to sit on the hairdresser's chair."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He pays regular visits to village shops.\n【定位】He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village. He'll just take himself\ninto any shop, anywhere.\n他的大部分时间都泡在村里的店铺和商家里，他会自顾自地走进任何一家店，到处都是。\n【信号】频次副词 most of his time 与范围词 any/anywhere 强调常态。He spends most of his\ntime in shops and businesses around the village 里的 most of his time 与紧接着的\nHe'll just take himself into any shop, anywhere 中的 any/anywhere，都是高强度的频\n次与范围信号，明确告诉考生「经常去店铺」是这只猫的常态行为——这正是 B 项 regular\nvisits 的答案信号。\n【替换】spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village ↔\npays regular visits to village shops.\n频次副词 most of his time 与 regular 等值\nshops and businesses around the village ↔ village shops\n村庄里的店铺这一对象一致\nmaking a regular appearance at local shops ↔ pays regular visits to village shops.\nregular appearance 与 regular visits 直接对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 He is a bold and aggressive pet. 盗用了录音里 Wilbur is both bold and friendly\n中的 bold，但把后半句的 friendly 偷偷换成了 aggressive。bold 在 friendly 与\naggressive 两种语境下意思走向完全相反，原文明确说他是 bold and friendly（大胆又友\n好），与 aggressive 截然相反。\n借用原文：Wilbur is both bold and friendly\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 He once bit a doctor's receptionist. 借用了录音里 the doctor's receptionist came\nback and he was sitting on her chair 中 receptionist 这一原词，但原文说的是「前台回\n来时发现他坐在自己椅子上」，根本没有「咬人」这一情节。bite 是凭「猫与人接触」自行\n加戏的虚构事件。\n借用原文：the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 He likes to sit on the hairdresser's chair. 把「坐在椅子上」这一情节（原文里发生\n在 the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair 即医生前台的\n椅子上）安到了理发店的椅子上。原文只在 Among his favorite haunts are a local\nhairdresser 里提及理发店是他常去的地方之一，但并未说他喜欢坐理发店椅子。\n借用原文：Among his favorite haunts are a local hairdresser", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the pub owner, Lesley Harper, say about the cat?", "options": ["A) He is fond of luxury cat food.", "B) He likes to stay in villagers' houses.", "C) He knows everybody in the village.", "D) He often seeks food around her pub."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is fond of luxury cat food.\n【定位】She also said he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high priced cat food.\n她还说他是一只品味奢侈的猫，喜欢吃高价猫粮。\n【信号】She also said 引出直接信息源加 expensive/high priced 同向强化。She also said he is\na cat of expensive tastes who likes his high priced cat food 用 She also said 直接把信\n息归到 Lesley Harper 名下，且 expensive tastes 与 high priced 在「奢侈高档」这一语\n义维度上同向强化，正是 A 项 luxury 的答案信号。\n【替换】a cat of expensive tastes ↔ He is fond of luxury cat food.\nexpensive tastes 与 fond of luxury 同义概括\nlikes his high priced cat food ↔ fond of luxury cat food\nhigh priced=luxury 的近义替换\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n选项 He likes to stay in villagers' houses. 借了录音中 happy for customers and\nvillagers young and old to come in and say hello 里的 villagers 一词，但原文说的是\n「乐于让村民和顾客进店打招呼」——他待的是酒馆，村民是进店打招呼的人，「待在村民\n家里」这一情节子虚乌有。\n借用原文：happy for customers and villagers young and old to come in and say hello\nC) 过度推断\n选项 He knows everybody in the village. 把录音里 happy for customers and villagers\nyoung and old to come in and say hello 这种「乐于和顾客与村民打招呼」的描述夸大成\n「认识村里每个人」。原文从未说他「认识」任何人，更没说 everybody，是典型的范围极\n大化推断。\n借用原文：happy for customers and villagers young and old to come in and say hello\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 He often seeks food around her pub. 借了酒馆这一场景设定，但 Lesley Harper 从\n未说他「在酒馆附近觅食」。原文里关于「食物」的唯一说法是 high priced cat food，重\n点在「高档」而非「在酒馆附近找」。seeks food around her pub 是把场景与食物两个真\n实元素硬拼出的虚构动作。\n借用原文：high priced cat food", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What are the speakers talking about in this conversation?", "options": ["A) Who to order the food.", "B) Whether to have sandwiches.", "C) When to go for their meal.", "D) Where to have their lunch."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Where to have their lunch.\n【定位】M: So, where do you want to go for lunch?\n男：那你想去哪儿吃午饭？\n【信号】开门见山的话题问句。where do you want to go for lunch? 是对话的第一句，用 So 起头\n直接抛话题，正是听力主旨题最常用的开头点题信号。后续整段所有讨论（三明治店、汉堡\n店、意大利餐厅）都是围绕这句话在选地方吃午饭。\n【替换】where do you want to go for lunch? ↔ Where to have their lunch.\nwhere to go 与 where to have 同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n全篇对话围绕选餐厅展开，从头到尾没有任何一句提到由谁来点食物，纯属 Who to order\nthe food. 制造的无中生有。\nB)\n录音里 What about the Sandwich Place on Camden Street? 与 I don't feel like\nsandwiches today. 确实念到了 sandwiches，Whether to have sandwiches. 就盗用了这\n个原词，让「听到原词就想选」的考生中招。但实际上三明治只是男方第一个提议的备选之\n一，被否决后整段对话又讨论了汉堡、意大利餐厅，三明治远不足以代表主旨。\n借用原文：I don't feel like sandwiches today.\nC)\n录音里 I need to get back by 2:30 for a conference call. 出现了具体时间，但那是女方解\n释自己时间紧、不要选服务慢的店的附带理由，整段对话从未围绕「几点去吃」展开讨论，\nWhen to go for their meal. 把次要约束当主线。\n借用原文：I need to get back by 2:30 for a conference call.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "Where did the speakers see Brigette Clark, the famous movie star?", "options": ["A) In the shopping center nearby.", "B) In the expensive Italian style diner.", "C) At the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.", "D) At the American restaurant they frequent."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） At the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.\n【定位】M: That's true. Remember that's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star.\n男：确实。记得吗？我们就是在那儿见过 Brigette Clark，那个有名的电影明星。\n【信号】that's where + 回指。that's where we saw Brigette Clark 用 that's where 把「那个地\n方」指回上一句男声提到的 the Sandwich Place on Camden Street，回指词 that 是细节\n定位的关键信号——它不是新地点，而是回指刚出现过的那个地点。\n【替换】the Sandwich Place on Camden Street ↔ At the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.\n原句锁回指对象\nthat's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star ↔\nAt the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.\n回指 that = 三明治店\n【排除】A)\n录音里只在第 7 段提到 on the way to the mall（去商场路上）作为美式餐厅的位置描述，\n但商场本身从未作为见明星的地点出现，In the shopping center nearby. 属于无中生有。\n借用原文：on the way to the mall\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音里 We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. 与 I haven't,\nbut I've noticed that it has great reviews 都明确说 Mario's 是他们还没去过的新意餐\n厅，与「在那儿见过明星」在逻辑上完全冲突，In the expensive Italian style diner. 盗用\n了 Italian 这个原词制造干扰。\n借用原文：Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner\nD)\n录音里 There's that American style diner on the way to the mall. 出现了 American 这\n个原词，At the American restaurant they frequent. 就盗用它制造干扰；但原文从未说他\n们常去那家美式餐厅，而且这家餐厅是男方拿来作为今天午饭的备选，与见明星这件已发生\n事毫无关联。\n借用原文：There's that American style diner on the way to the mall.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why does the woman say she needs to get back by 2:30?", "options": ["A) There is to be a conference call.", "B) She has to meet with her boss then.", "C) There will be crowds of people waiting for her.", "D) She will have a photo taken with Brigette Clark."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） There is to be a conference call.\n【定位】W: I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time. And I need to\nget back by 2:30 for a conference call.\n女：我喜欢那家。他们的薯条很棒，但上菜慢。而且我得在 2:30 之前赶回去开电话会议。\n【信号】for + 原因短语。I need to get back by 2:30 for a conference call. 中的 for 是原因介\n词，直接把 2:30 这个时间点和「开电话会议」这个理由焊死。Why 类题听到\nfor/because/so that 就要立刻锁定，理由就在它后面。\n【替换】for a conference call ↔ There is to be a conference call.\nfor + 名词转 there is to be\n【排除】B)\n录音从头到尾没有任何 boss、manager、meet with 之类的词，She has to meet with\nher boss then. 完全是无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her. 里确实出\n现了 crowds of people，There will be crowds of people waiting for her. 就盗用了这个\n原词；但原文里那群人围的是 Brigette Clark，不是女方，C 把围观对象张冠李戴到她身\n上，且时态从过去 were 偷换成将来 will be。\n借用原文：There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her.\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音 asking for a photo with her 出现 photo，Brigette Clark 出现人名，She will have\na photo taken with Brigette Clark. 把这两个原词拼成「女方将与 Brigette Clark 合影」\n的新事件——这件事原文从未发生，是典型的原词拼接陷阱。\n借用原文：asking for a photo with her", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why does the woman say Jeremy should throw his soup in the bin?", "options": ["A) She doesn't deem homemade soup tasty.", "B) She doesn't think his wife cooks well.", "C) She feels Jeremy would rather dine out.", "D) She has found the soup smells terrible."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） She doesn't think his wife cooks well.\n【定位】W: Poor Jeremy. His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.\n女：可怜的 Jeremy。他妻子是个糟糕的厨师。他真该把那碗汤扔了，跟我们一起去。\n【信号】终判式因果链。His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin 是一\n个语义上的因果链：先下评判 His wife is a terrible cook，再据此建议 He should\nthrow…。前一句就是建议的真正原因，是答案句中最关键的承载句。\n【替换】His wife is a terrible cook. ↔ She doesn't think his wife cooks well.\n贬义形容词转含蓄否定\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音里女方的批评对象是 His wife（人 - 妻子的厨艺），不是汤本身。She doesn't deem\nhomemade soup tasty. 把矛头从「妻子厨艺差」偷换成「她觉得家常汤本身不好吃」，是\n范围偏移。她没有否定家常汤这个品类，只是否定他妻子做的这一碗。\n借用原文：His wife is a terrible cook.\nC)\n录音 He should throw that soup in the bin and join us. 出现了 join us，She feels\nJeremy would rather dine out. 就据此过度推断成「她觉得 Jeremy 更愿意出去吃」。但\n女方建议他出来吃是她的提议，不是 Jeremy 自己的偏好；原文里 Jeremy 自己说的是 He\nhas brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in.，恰恰是要留下不出去。C 把女方的\n建议错认成 Jeremy 的意愿，过度推断且方向反了。\n借用原文：He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里女方说的是 His wife is a terrible cook.——评价做菜人的水平，从未提到汤闻起来\n怎么样。She has found the soup smells terrible. 盗用了 terrible 这个原词，但把它从修\n饰 cook 偷换为修饰 smells，是经典的「原词换搭配」陷阱。\n借用原文：His wife is a terrible cook.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Who is the man talking to in the conversation?", "options": ["A) A landlady.", "B) A waitress.", "C) A receptionist.", "D) A saleswoman."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A receptionist.\n【定位】Hi, there. How are you today? Do you have a reservation with us already?\n你好，今天感觉如何？您已经在我们这里预订了吗？\n【信号】开场专属话术。Do you have a reservation with us already? 是酒店前台接待客人时最典\n型的开场询问——问『是否有预订』。这种『询问预订』的话术是接待员的专属标记，是定\n身份的信号句。\n【替换】Do you have a reservation with us already? ↔ A receptionist.\n话术 → 身份的同义替换\nI can get you all checked in and show you to your rooms. ↔ A receptionist.\n结尾办理入住二次确认\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA landlady. 是出租房屋的房东，话术应是 rent/deposit/lease，不会用 reservation 这类\n酒店术语；录音里也没有出现任何与租房相关的词。\nB) 原词陷阱\nA waitress. 是餐厅服务员。录音 has all kinds of food and cool little shops 确实提到了\nfood，但讲的是夜市，不是接待方提供餐饮服务，属于典型原词陷阱——听到 food 就误以\n为对方是 waitress。\n借用原文：has all kinds of food and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and\nclothing made by hand\nD) 原词陷阱\nA saleswoman. 是售货员。录音 cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry\nand clothing made by hand 确实提到了『卖纪念品和手工衣物的小店』，但卖东西的是\n夜市的小店，不是这位接待者，属于典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do we learn about the man's father-in-law from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He was involved in a terrible car accident last April.", "B) He has much difficulty getting up and down stairs.", "C) He is expected to undergo a knee operation.", "D) He prefers to stay next door to the children."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He has much difficulty getting up and down stairs.\n【定位】My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee\noperation last April.\n我岳父自从去年四月做了膝关节手术以来，上下楼梯一直有很大困难。\n【信号】现在完成时 + since 圈定当前状态。has had terrible problems getting up and down\nstairs since his knee operation last April 中 has had 是现在完成时，配合 since…，明确\n这是『从手术之后一直延续到现在的状况』。手术（knee operation）是过去事件、上下楼\n梯困难（getting up and down stairs）才是当前状态——也就是题目要问的『现在我们了\n解到他怎么样』。\n【替换】has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs ↔\nHe has much difficulty getting up and down stairs.\nterrible problems = much difficulty\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe was involved in a terrible car accident last April. 盗用了录音里的 terrible 与 last\nApril 这两个高辨识度词，制造『我好像听见 terrible 和 last April』的错觉，但录音里 last\nApril 修饰的是 knee operation（膝关节手术），不是 car accident（车祸）；车祸录音里\n根本没提。\n借用原文：has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee operation last April\nC) 与原文相反\nHe is expected to undergo a knee operation. 用 is expected to undergo（将要做）描\n述膝关节手术，但录音里 since his knee operation last April 说的是『自从去年四月做了\n手术之后』——手术已经做完。时态从过去/完成偷换成将来，与原文相反。\n借用原文：since his knee operation last April\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe prefers to stay next door to the children. 借用了录音 right next to each other on\nthe ground floor 里 next to 的安排关系，但录音说的是『前台把两间房安排在彼此隔\n壁』，并不是岳父 prefers（更愿意）住孩子隔壁，主语与动机都被替换。\n借用原文：right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that\nthey have trouble with stairs", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "Why does the man say they will explore all the options available?", "options": ["A) To please his parents-in-law.", "B) To find the best trip for his kids.", "C) To satisfy his curiosity.", "D) To compare prices."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To compare prices.\n【定位】We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure. So,\nyes, that would be very helpful in comparing prices.\n我们本来打算租辆车，但我们一定会把所有可选方案都看一遍。所以，是的，这对比价会很\n有帮助。\n【信号】So + that would be very helpful in...。So, yes, that would be very helpful in\ncomparing prices. 中 So 是结果连词，紧跟前一句『会把所有方案都看一遍』，that\nwould be very helpful in 直接说明这样做的好处/目的——comparing prices（比价）。\n这就是男方自报动机的关键句。\n【替换】that would be very helpful in comparing prices ↔ To compare prices.\nbe helpful in V-ing → To do\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo please his parents-in-law. 利用了录音 another double room for your parents-in-\nlaw 里 parents-in-law 这个原词，让人误以为『探索所有方案是为了照顾岳父母』，但录\n音里只说他们『住一楼』，从未把『探索方案』的动机归到讨好岳父母上。\n借用原文：another double room for your parents-in-law\nB) 原词陷阱\nTo find the best trip for his kids. 利用了录音 The kids will love it 里出现的 kids 与 trip\n两个原词，制造『是为了给孩子找最佳旅程』的错觉。但 The kids will love it 是女方推荐\nboat trip 时说的，男方说『探索所有方案』的动机不是这件事。\n借用原文：The kids will love it and there are so many islands nearby to explore\nC) 未提及\nTo satisfy his curiosity. 录音里完全没有出现 curiosity 或与好奇心相关的表达；选项是凭\n空加的目的，属于未提及型干扰。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman suggest the man and his family do close to the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Visit a local art gallery.", "B) Go on a boat trip.", "C) Take some photos of the islands.", "D) Try her hand-made clothing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Go on a boat trip.\n【定位】I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it and there are so many\nislands nearby to explore.\n我会推荐你们去坐船出游一趟。孩子们会很喜欢，附近还有很多岛屿可以探索。\n【信号】I would recommend... 建议句。I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. 中 I\nwould recommend 是最显性的建议信号词，紧跟的动作 getting out on a boat trip 就是\n被建议的事。\n【替换】getting out on a boat trip ↔ Go on a boat trip.\ngetting out on = Go on\n【排除】A) 未提及\nVisit a local art gallery. 录音里完全没有提到 art gallery（艺术画廊）；女方推荐的是\nboat trip 和 night market，画廊纯属选项添加的迷惑活动。\nC) 原词陷阱\nTake some photos of the islands. 利用了录音 there are so many islands nearby to\nexplore 里出现的 islands 一词，让人以为『建议去拍岛屿照片』。但原文里 islands 是\nboat trip 时『可以探索』的对象，女方从未建议『拍照』；take photos 这个动作录音里\n没有出现。\n借用原文：there are so many islands nearby to explore\nD) 张冠李戴\nTry her hand-made clothing. 利用了录音 clothing made by hand 这个原词组，但录音\n里 clothing made by hand 是『夜市小店在卖』的东西，不是『她』（前台接待员）自己\n手工做的，也不是被建议的核心动作；同时 try（试穿/尝试）这个动作录音里也没有。\n借用原文：clothing made by hand", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the passage say about artificial intelligence agents?", "options": ["A) They are more intelligent than many of us.", "B) They have already become our new friends.", "C) They have begun to affect our social behavior.", "D) They play increasingly more important roles."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They play increasingly more important roles.\n【定位】Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives.\n人工智能代理在我们的生活中扮演着越来越具有影响力的角色。\n【信号】篇章首句即点题句。答案句正是录音的第一句 Artificial intelligence agents play ever\nmore influential roles in our lives.：篇章独白的开头第一句几乎一定是点题句，直接给出\n对象 + 总体评价，这是篇章听力定位最强的信号——比任何转折词都更稳。\n【替换】play ever more influential roles ↔ play increasingly more important roles\never more 与 increasingly 同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey are more intelligent than many of us. 利用了录音里 Artificial intelligence agents\n中 intelligence 这个词，把 \"人工智能\" 偷换成 \"它们比我们许多人更聪明\"。录音从未把 AI\n与人做智力比较，主体考生一听到 intelligence 就反射性挑 intelligent 是典型的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：Artificial intelligence agents\nB) 与原文相反\nThey have already become our new friends. 利用了录音里 suggesting new friends\n（替我们推荐新朋友），把 \"推荐朋友\" 偷换成 \"它们自己已经成为我们的新朋友\"。这是\nalready + 完成时把功能性服务夸大为既成事实，方向已经反了。\n借用原文：suggesting new friends\nC) 偷换概念\nThey have begun to affect our social behavior. 利用了录音里 play ever more\ninfluential roles in our lives（在生活中起越来越重要的作用），把宽泛的 \"生活\" 偷换成专\n指的 social behavior（社交行为）。录音从未提及 social behavior 这一具体范畴，属于把\n宽口径收窄到一个录音没说过的窄范畴。\n借用原文：play ever more influential roles in our lives", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the new study want to find out about using an artificial intelligence agent?", "options": ["A) Whether it might have any effect on the way we negotiate.", "B) Whether it might actually outperform human negotiators.", "C) Whether it can facilitate business transactions.", "D) Whether it can speed up legal procedures."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Whether it might have any effect on the way we negotiate.\n【定位】So, it's important to know whether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how\nwe negotiate.\n因此，弄清楚使用人工智能代理是否会影响我们谈判的方式，是很重要的。\n【信号】So 引出结论 + know whether 引出研究问题。答案句以 So, it's important to know\nwhether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. 出现：\nSo 是结论信号，it's important to know whether… 是研究问题的固定起手式——这是篇\n章里 \"研究想搞清什么\" 最强的语言信号，几乎不会与别的句子搞混。\n【替换】might affect how we negotiate ↔ might have any effect on the way we negotiate\n动词 affect 与名词 effect 同根改写\nit's important to know whether ↔ Whether it might have any effect on the way we negotiate.\nWhether 从句被独立保留\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nWhether it might actually outperform human negotiators. 把研究问题从 \"是否影响我\n们的谈判方式\" 强行升级为 \"是否真的胜过人类谈判者\"。录音里只问 affect，没有任何\noutperform / beat / better than 的提法，是典型把 \"有影响\" 推到 \"胜过\" 的两步跳跃。\nC) 原词陷阱\nWhether it can facilitate business transactions. 直接搬用了录音里 commercial\ntransactions（商业交易）这个场景词。录音只是把 commercial transactions 列为 AI 代\n理可能介入的应用场景之一，并没有把它定为研究问题；选项把 \"场景\" 偷换成 \"研究问\n题\"，是典型听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：commercial transactions\nD) 原词陷阱\nWhether it can speed up legal procedures. 同样借用了录音里 legal disputes（法律纠\n纷）这个场景词，再把名词改成 legal procedures（法律程序）。录音没有提加快速度\n（speed up）这一研究目标，是 \"原词靠近 + 概念替换\" 的双重陷阱。\n借用原文：legal disputes", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What did participants tend to do when assigned an AI agent to negotiate on their behalf?", "options": ["A) Choose to be tough.", "B) Sympathize with their opponent.", "C) Use deceptive strategies.", "D) Appear to be pleasant."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Use deceptive strategies.\n【定位】Participants were more willing to employ deceptive strategies when assigned an agent to\nnegotiate on their behalf.\n当被安排一个代理替自己谈判时，参与者更愿意采用欺骗性策略。\n【信号】段末 were more willing to 是结论信号。答案句出现在录音末尾 Participants were more\nwilling to employ deceptive strategies when assigned an agent to negotiate on their\nbehalf.：段末 + were more willing to 是研究类篇章给出实验结论最稳的双重信号——前\n面 tough / sympathy / anger / dissatisfaction 都是为它做铺垫的策略列举，真正的结论\n压到最后一句。\n【替换】were more willing to employ deceptive strategies ↔ Use deceptive strategies.\nemploy 与 Use 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nChoose to be tough. 直接借用录音 participants could choose to be tough by making\nan opening demand far greater than what they'd be willing to accept 中 \"choose to\nbe tough\" 几个原词。但这一句只是研究方法里 \"参与者可以做出哪些选择\" 的举例之一，\n不是研究结论里 \"被安排代理时实际倾向做\" 的事。听到 tough 就选是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：participants could choose to be tough\nB) 原词陷阱\nSympathize with their opponent. 借用了录音 choose to express sympathy with their\nopponent to appear pleasant 里 sympathy with their opponent。这句同样是研究方法\n的举例选项之一，是受试者 \"可以选\" 的策略，不是结论性倾向；选项把 sympathy 改成\nsympathize 听起来更顺耳，但只是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：express sympathy with their opponent\nD) 原词陷阱\nAppear to be pleasant. 借用了录音 to appear pleasant 的几个字。但 to appear\npleasant 在录音里只是说明 \"表达同情\" 这种策略的目的（让自己看起来友好），仍属于研\n究方法的举例铺垫，并不是结论。把目的状语当作结论行为是典型 \"听到 pleasant 就选\" 的\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：to appear pleasant", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about the benefits the participants gained from the healthy cooking course?", "options": ["A) They were perceived differently by some academics.", "B) They still existed six months after the course ended.", "C) They varied greatly among the course participants.", "D) They were only measurable within seven weeks."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They still existed six months after the course ended.\n【定位】These benefits remained six months after the completion of the course.\n这些益处在课程结束六个月之后依然存在。\n【信号】段落收束句 + 时间锚点 six months。These benefits remained six months after the\ncompletion of the course. 是第二段最后一句，承担收束作用：前两句 Course\nparticipants saw significant improvements in general health. 与 They also reported\nimproved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program. 先讲\n了课程结束后“立即”的好处，最后一句一个 remained + six months 把时间窗拉长——这\n正是预读时圈出的时间锚点。\n【替换】These benefits remained six months after the completion of the course. ↔\nThey still existed six months after the course ended.\nremained = still existed；completion of the course = course ended\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项里 perceived differently by some academics 听起来像在说“被某些学者看法不一”，\n它盗用了第一段里 Academics measured the program's effect on cooking confidence\nand self-perceived mental health. 出现过的 Academics 与 self-perceived，但录音从未\n说 academics 之间对这些好处有不同看法，是把原文里出现过的词重新拼装成一个新意\n思。\n借用原文：Academics measured the program's effect on cooking confidence and self-perceived\nmental health.\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 They varied greatly among the course participants. 说好处在参与者之间差异巨\n大，但第四段 Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who\nwere overweight or obese and those in a healthy weight range. 反而说好处在不同体\n重人群之间是相等的，方向恰好与本选项相反。\n借用原文：the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or\nobese and those in a healthy weight range\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 They were only measurable within seven weeks. 表面有 seven weeks 这个录音里\n念到的数字（a seven-week healthy cooking course），听起来像是“原话”。但 seven-\nweek 在录音中只是课程的时长，不是好处的可测时间窗；而且加了 only 限定，与第二段\n的 six months 直接矛盾。\n借用原文：a seven-week healthy cooking course", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What do the new research findings suggest about mental health benefits?", "options": ["A) They can be easily seen among participants in a healthy weight range.", "B) They should be attributed to participants' change in diet behaviors.", "C) They are linked to cooking confidence and cooking satisfaction.", "D) They actually result from eating more fruits and vegetables."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They are linked to cooking confidence and cooking satisfaction.\n【定位】This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking, and mental\nhealth benefits.\n这一点表明，烹饪信心与对烹饪的满足感和心理健康益处之间存在关联。\n【信号】转折 However 之后的 This suggests 结论句。第四段首句 However, the study showed\nparticipants' mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after\ncompleting the program. 用 however 翻盘——把第三段“也许是因为多吃果蔬”的猜测推\n翻；段尾 This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around\ncooking, and mental health benefits. 用 This suggests 直接给出研究真正归因。这两个\n信号（however + This suggests）是听力定位结论句的金标准。\n【替换】This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around cooking, and mental health\nbenefits.\n↔ They are linked to cooking confidence and cooking satisfaction.\na link between A and B = A are linked to B\nsatisfaction around cooking ↔ cooking satisfaction\nof/around 短语 ↔ 复合名词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 They can be easily seen among participants in a healthy weight range. 把录音里\nthe mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or\nobese and those in a healthy weight range 中的 healthy weight range 摘出来，但录\n音强调的是“无论超重还是健康体重，效果都相等”——选项却暗示“只在健康体重组里更容易\n看到”，把“相等”偷换成“在某一组更明显”。\n借用原文：the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or\nobese and those in a healthy weight range\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 They should be attributed to participants' change in diet behaviors. 把心理健康\n益处归给“饮食行为的改变”，但 participants' mental health improved even if their\nreported diet did not change after completing the program. 直接说“即便饮食没变，\n心理健康也改善了”——选项与原文方向恰好相反。\n借用原文：participants' mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after\ncompleting the program\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 They actually result from eating more fruits and vegetables. 几乎逐字盗用\nResearchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and\nvegetables, and improved longer term mental health.，听感非常“对”。但录音明确指出\n这只是“之前的研究”和“可能的解释”，被第四段 however 推翻；本研究真正的结论是烹饪信\n心与满足感，而不是多吃果蔬。\n借用原文：Researchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and vegetables,\nand improved longer term mental health.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What plays a role in determining who benefits most from learning to cook?", "options": ["A) Gender.", "B) Confidence.", "C) Health.", "D) Practice."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Gender.\n【定位】Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part.\n谁从学做饭中获益最多？性别在其中起了作用。\n【信号】段首设问 + 直接应答。Who benefits most from learning to cook? 是段首设问，几乎是\n把题干 What plays a role in determining who benefits most from learning to cook?\n原样念了一遍——这种设问句之后的第一句 Gender plays a part. 就是答案句。设问+应答\n是篇章独白里最强的答案信号。\n【替换】Gender plays a part. ↔ Gender.\n原词直出，无需改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Confidence. 与录音 77 per cent of female participants were confident about\ncooking, but just 23 per cent of males were confident. 中反复出现的\nconfident/confidence 同根——录音确实在大量谈“信心”，但 confidence 是“差异表现的\n指标”而非“决定谁受益最多”的那个变量；真正决定的是性别（女生先有信心、男生后追\n上）。\n借用原文：cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Health. 利用了全文反复出现的 health/mental health/general health 主题词的熟悉\n感，但录音从未把 health 作为“决定谁受益最多”的变量；恰恰相反，the mental health\nbenefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese and those in\na healthy weight range 还说健康状态不同的人受益是相等的。\n借用原文：Course participants saw significant improvements in general health.\nD) 未提及\n选项 Practice. 的 practice（练习/实践）在录音中没有任何对应：第五段讨论性别差异和家\n庭烹饪平衡，从未把“练习多少”列为决定受益的变量。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does research show about our personal space?", "options": ["A) It keeps others away.", "B) It causes discomfort.", "C) It remains visible.", "D) It varies in size."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It varies in size.\n【定位】Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes.\n然而，研究表明，我们实际上拥有不同大小的（个人空间）气泡。\n【信号】Research shows + however 转折。答案句一头连了两个强信号：Research shows 是题\n干 What does research show 的原句对应，however 是转折信号，转折之后才是真意\n——前半『以为是同一个气泡』被推翻，后半『其实大小不同』才是结论。\n【替换】bubbles of different sizes ↔ It varies in size.\nbubbles of different sizes = varies in size\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It keeps others away. 借用了原文 others can't enter 里的 others 来制造『听到\nothers 就想选』的诱惑；但原文说的是『别人进不来（指气泡的功能描述）』，并不是『研\n究发现』那条结论。研究发现的真意在转折 however 之后，不在转折之前。\n借用原文：others can't enter\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 It causes discomfort. 抓住了原文 without causing discomfort 里的 discomfort 来\n制造原词陷阱；但原文是说『别人进入气泡会带来不适』，描述的是『进入气泡的后果』，\n不是研究本身的结论。这条信息在 23 题才用得上，放到 22 题就是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：without causing discomfort\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 It remains visible. 说气泡『一直可见』，而原文 an invisible bubble of space 明确\n说 invisible（看不见）。选项盗用了气泡这个话题，却把极性反过来了，是典型的极性反转\n陷阱。\n借用原文：an invisible bubble of space", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What happens if a mere acquaintance enters our intimate space?", "options": ["A) It makes us feel uncomfortable.", "B) It renders the acquaintance a stranger.", "C) It brings the acquaintance closer to us.", "D) It causes the bubble around us to vanish."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It makes us feel uncomfortable.\n【定位】A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes us uncomfortable.\n一个仅仅是熟人的人进入我们的亲密空间，会让我们感到不舒服。\n【信号】题干定位词原句重现。录音里 A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space 几乎是\n题干 a mere acquaintance enters our intimate space 的原句搬运，定位词全中；其后紧\n跟的 makes us uncomfortable 就是直白结果，是典型的『触发条件 + 紧跟结果』结构。\n【替换】makes us uncomfortable ↔ It makes us feel uncomfortable.\n几乎逐词对应\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n选项 It renders the acquaintance a stranger. 借用录音里反复出现的 acquaintance 和\nstrangers 来制造『听到原词就选』的诱惑；但原文从未说『熟人会变成陌生人』，只是分\n别为不同人群分配了不同空间。从『让人不适』推到『熟人变陌生人』属于过度推断。\n借用原文：Friends and acquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 It brings the acquaintance closer to us. 说『把熟人拉近』，但原文 makes us\nuncomfortable 明确说会让我们不适，属于结果方向反了。同时它盗用了 acquaintance 这\n个原词来骗你选。\n借用原文：makes us uncomfortable\nD) 未提及\n选项 It causes the bubble around us to vanish. 借用整组核心词 bubble 来吸引点击，但\n录音从未说过『熟人进入亲密空间会让气泡消失』，只是说会让我们不舒服。bubble\nvanish 这一结果属于原文未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Where do people feel comfortable interacting with new acquaintances or strangers?", "options": ["A) In personal space.", "B) In social space.", "C) Within a distance of 18 inches.", "D) Over 2 feet away from one another."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） In social space.\n【定位】Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is \"social space\". Here people feel comfortable\nconducting routine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers.\n从距离我们 4 到 12 英尺的范围被称为『社交空间』。在这里，人们与新熟人或完全陌生的\n人进行日常社交互动时感到舒适。\n【信号】题干关键词原句重现 + 空间命名句。录音里 new acquaintances or total strangers 几乎\n与题干 new acquaintances or strangers 一字不差对应；这句又紧跟在 \"social space\" 的\n定义之后，定位极其稳。\n【替换】\"social space\" ↔ In social space.\n专有概念名直接对应\nroutine social interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers ↔\ninteracting with new acquaintances or strangers\n行为限定原句对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 In personal space. 借用了原文中的 personal space 这个原词，但录音里 personal\nspace 是给『朋友和熟人』用的（friends and acquaintances 在『informal\nconversations』里使用），并不是给陌生人用的。配的人群对不上。\n借用原文：Friends and acquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during\ninformal conversations, but strangers are forbidden.\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Within a distance of 18 inches. 盗用了录音 18 inches 这个原词数字，但原文里 18\ninches 是给 intimate space 的尺寸（只允许家人、宠物和最亲密的朋友进入），与题干问\n的『陌生人交往的距离』完全不是一回事。\n借用原文：18 inches in every direction\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Over 2 feet away from one another. 里的 2 feet 看起来很像录音里反复出现的几个\n距离数字，但录音里给陌生人/新熟人互动的距离是 4 to 12 feet，不是 2 feet。选项偷偷把\n数字换小了，是典型的数字偷换。\n借用原文：Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is \"social space\"", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "When are the sizes of our bubbles fixed?", "options": ["A) When we begin to feel fear.", "B) When we develop a sense of space.", "C) When we are 3 or 4 years old.", "D) When we enter our teens."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） When we enter our teens.\n【定位】The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens.\n我们（个人空间）气泡的大小在我们进入青少年时期时就被定型了。\n【信号】题干原句重现 + 关键介词 by。录音 The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens 几\n乎逐词复述题干 the sizes of our bubbles fixed，再加上时间介词 by our teens，定位与\n时间一并锁定。这是题干原句重现 + 时间限定词紧跟的双信号。\n【替换】by our teens ↔ When we enter our teens.\n介词短语 ↔ 时间状语从句\nare fixed ↔ fixed\n动作原词重现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 When we begin to feel fear. 盗用了录音 the brain region involved in fear 里的\nfear 来吸引点击，但原文从未把 fear 当作一个时间节点，只是顺带提到大脑里负责恐惧的\n区域参与构建气泡。这是把『脑区话题』偷换成『时间节点』。\n借用原文：These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the brain region involved in fear.\nB) 答非所问\n选项 When we develop a sense of space. 盗用了录音 we begin to develop our\nindividual sense of personal space 里的 develop 和 sense of space，但这一句对应的动\n作是 develop（开始发展），不是题干问的 fixed（被定型）。同一段里两个动作各配一个\n时间，选项把动作和时间故意错配。\n借用原文：we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around age 3 or 4\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 When we are 3 or 4 years old. 直接盗用了录音里的数字 around age 3 or 4，但这\n个年龄绑的是『开始发展空间感』(develop)，不是『定型』(fixed)。题干问的是 fixed 的\n时间，故 3 or 4 岁是错配。\n借用原文：around age 3 or 4", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) surprisingly\n副词\n【词性槽】形容词 little 前缺修饰语，填副词\n【依据】呼应 However 的转折与意外感\nHowever, there has been __(26)__ little research on the influence of leisure\nactivities：However 表明\"家庭对工作影响研究很多，却出人意料地少有关于休闲的研\n究\"——surprisingly（令人惊讶地）正强调这种\"本该有却很少\"的反差。\n【竞争词】C) casually 语义不搭（随意地）\ncasually\"随意地\"修饰人做事的方式，casually little research 讲不通——研究数量\"少\"与\"随\n意\"无关，逻辑不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) shaped\n动词\n【词性槽】从句主语后缺谓语动词\n【依据】对应全文核心动词\"影响/塑造\"\nto look at how the time spent on their hobbies __(27)__ their work life呼应开篇 how\nfamily life can affect productivity——研究的正是爱好时间如何\"影响、塑造\"工作生活。\nshape（塑造、影响）与 affect 同义，是本句最贴的谓语动词。\n【竞争词】M) scratch 词义不搭\nscratch\"抓挠\"，scratch their work life 语义荒谬，与\"研究爱好如何影响工作\"无关，纯靠词性\n蒙的人最易在此掉坑。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— 纯靠词性蒙易撞上 scratch。第 27 空 how the time\nspent on their hobbies __(27)__ their work life需及物动词，若不读懂\"研究爱好如何影响\n工作\"的语境、只凭\"是个动词\"乱填，scratch 这种词性也对的词最易误选；实则 scratch\ntheir work life 语义荒谬。防坑：动词空选定前务必把动宾连读一遍，看搭配是否成话。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) normal\n形容词\n【词性槽】than 后跟比较基准，此处填名词化形容词\n【依据】than normal=\"比平常更久\"\nparticipants spent longer than __(28)__：longer than normal 是地道说法，意为\"花的时\n间比平常（正常水平）更长\"，normal 正好充当\"平常\"这个比较基准，与下文\"花更久会增\n强工作信心\"衔接。\n【竞争词】K) relative 被 longer 诱导但不成基准\nrelative\"相对的/亲戚\"，句中有比较级 longer 易让人误以为\"相对\"很搭，但 longer than\nrelative 既不表常态也不成搭配，无法作\"比平常更久\"的基准。\nJ) prevalent 义为\"普遍的\"，不搭\nprevalent\"普遍流行的\"，longer than prevalent 讲不通，与时间长短的比较基准无关。\nL) removed 义为\"远离的\"，留给 35 空\nremoved\"远离的\"用于 far removed from，longer than removed 不通；它是第 35 空的\n答案。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 K) —— relative 被比较级 longer 诱导。第 28 空 spent longer\nthan __(28)__有比较级 longer，relative（相对的）让人误以为\"相对而言\"很搭，词性也是\n形容词；但 longer than 后需要的是\"常态\"基准 normal，longer than relative 根本不成\n话。防坑：than 后填\"和什么比\"的基准，认准 normal/usual。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) case\n名词\n【词性槽】the + ___ 作 was 的表语，缺名词\n【依据】the case=\"实情如此\"固定表达\nBut this was only the __(29)__ when…：this was only the case when… 意为\"只有在…\n时才是这种情况\"，the case 是地道固定搭配，承接上句\"花更久会增强信心\"并加上限定\n条件。\n【竞争词】E) estate 词义离题\nestate\"房地产\"，the estate 与本句\"这只在…时成立\"的逻辑毫无关系。\nG) faculty 义偏不搭\nfaculty\"教员/才能\"，this was the faculty 不成话，无法表\"情况如此\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) casually\n副词\n【词性槽】句末修饰 did it 的方式状语\n【依据】与 only 呼应\"只是随便做做\"\nhobby was similar to their work but they only did it __(30)__与前半句 a serious hobby\n（认真的爱好）对照：but they only（只是）暗示这里是\"不认真、随便玩玩\"，casually\n（随意地）正好。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) efficiency\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 work 后缺名词作宾语中心\n【依据】与 work 搭配且贴合\"损耗工作表现\"主线\ndecreased their work __(31)__承接上句\"爱好既认真又与工作相似时，多花时间反而坏\n事\"。work efficiency（工作效率）是地道搭配，\"降低了工作效率\"正是这种损耗的体现，\n与 productivity（生产力）一脉相承。\n【竞争词】B) case 搭配与逻辑都不合\ncase\"情况\"，decreased their work case 不成搭配，\"减少工作情况\"逻辑讲不通；case 该留给\nthe case 那种框架（第 29 空）。\nE) estate 词义离题（房地产）\nestate意为房地产、庄园，work estate 不成搭配，更与全文\"爱好—工作信心\"主题毫无关系，\n纯属凑数干扰。\nG) faculty 义偏且不搭\nfaculty常指大学教员或人的官能/才能，work faculty 不是地道说法，也无法承接\"多花时间在爱\n好上反而减少了什么\"的语境，逻辑接不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) exhausted\n形容词\n【词性槽】be left + ___ 表状态，需形容词\n【依据】承接\"耗尽心理资源\"\n前句说维持认真的爱好要 invest significant psychological resources，若爱好与工作要求\n相同，人就 left __(32)__ and unable to perform well——被掏空、累垮，exhausted（精\n疲力竭的）与\"and 后无法好好工作\"因果相承。\n【竞争词】I) normal 义为\"正常\"，与负面状态相反\nnormal\"正常的\"，be left normal and unable to perform well 自相矛盾——既正常又无法胜\n任，逻辑冲突；normal 该留给\"比平常\"的比较基准（第 28 空）。\nJ) prevalent 义为\"普遍的\"，不描述人\nprevalent\"普遍流行的\"形容现象，不能形容\"人被弄成…的状态\"，left prevalent 讲不通。\nK) relative 义为\"相对的\"，不搭\nrelative\"相对的/亲戚\"，be left relative 既不表状态也无意义，与\"无法胜任工作\"接不上。\nL) removed 义为\"远离的\"，留给 35 空\nremoved\"远离的\"用于 far removed from，单独 left removed 不表\"累垮\"这种状态，它是第\n35 空 far removed from 的答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) interfere\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may not 后缺动词原形\n【依据】对应\"不会以同样方式妨碍\"\n本句与上句构成转折：上句说相似爱好会把人掏空，But if their hobby is quite different\nfrom their career, it may not __(33)__ in the same way——若爱好与工作不同，就\"不会\n以同样方式干扰\"工作。interfere（干扰、妨碍）正好顶上这个\"不会妨碍\"，且 interfere 不\n及物，正合空后直接接 in the same way（无宾语）。\n【竞争词】A) boost 语义方向反（褒义）\nboost\"提升\"是褒义，但 may not 否定 + But 转折要的是\"不会造成（负面）干扰\"，填 boost\n成了\"不会提升\"，与\"帮助发展技能\"的下文自相矛盾。\nM) scratch 词义不搭\nscratch\"抓挠\"，may not scratch in the same way 语义荒谬，与\"不会以同样方式妨碍工\n作\"无关。\nN) shaped 中性\"塑造\"接不上否定转折\nshaped\"塑造\"中性，may not shape in the same way（不会以同样方式塑造）与上句\"耗尽资\n源\"的负面对照对不上；这里要的是负面动作的反面（不会干扰）。shaped 是第 27 空的答案。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) boost\n动词\n【词性槽】that 引导定语从句缺谓语动词\n【依据】与 confidence 正搭，承\"积极作用\"语境\n本句讲不同类型的爱好能 help them develop other knowledge and skills，进而\"增强工\n作中的信心\"。boost one's confidence（提升信心）是固定正向搭配，与 help develop 的\n积极语气一致。\n【竞争词】M) scratch 词义不搭（抓挠）\nscratch意为抓、挠或\"从零开始（from scratch）\"，scratch their confidence 不成立，与\"增强\n信心\"毫无关系，是形义双重不搭的干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) boost", "B) case", "C) casually", "D) efficiency", "E) estate", "F) exhausted", "G) faculty", "H) interfere", "I) normal", "J) prevalent", "K) relative", "L) removed", "M) scratch", "N) shaped", "O) surprisingly"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) removed\n形容词\n【词性槽】so far ___ from 中作形容词\n【依据】far removed from=\"与…相去甚远\"固定搭配\nSince climbing is so far __(35)__ from their day-to-day work activities：far removed\nfrom 是固定搭配，意为\"与日常工作活动相去甚远\"，呼应下文 they can still recover from\nthe demands of their job——正因差距大才能恢复精力。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "In the absence of Federal legislation, some states in the US have passed laws concerning paid family leave.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】州立法填补联邦空缺\n【定位】The US is one of only three countries in the world not to offer statutory(法定的) paid leave,\nbut increasingly states and companies are starting to take up the issue. So far, eight states and\nthe District of Columbia have their own paid family leave laws.\n美国是全球仅有的三个不提供法定带薪假的国家之一，但越来越多的州和公司开始着手处理\n这个问题。迄今为止，已有八个州加上哥伦比亚特区拥有自己的带薪家庭假法律。\n【改写】In the absence of Federal legislation ↔\none of only three countries in the world not to offer statutory\\(法定的\\) paid leave\n缺联邦立法即无全国法定假\nsome states in the US have passed laws concerning paid family leave ↔\neight states and the District of Columbia have their own paid family leave laws\n八个州即some states\n【辨邻】 H H段是 put pressure on companies to make their parental leave policies gender\nneutral，主体是公司政策，没有任何\"州通过法律\"的陈述，故不是源头段。\nlegal cases against companies involving paternity leave", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Most fathers admitted that even during their paternity leave they actually did much less childcare than the mother.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】父亲承认照护远少于母亲\n【定位】While about 75% of employees said both genders should give the same amount of care, the\nmajority of men and nearly half of women admitted that in reality the female actually did\nmost of it. A tiny fraction, 2%, of men said they did more of the childcare.\n尽管约75%的员工说两性应付出同等照护，但多数男性和近半女性承认，现实中女性实际承\n担了大部分照护。只有极小一部分、2%的男性说自己承担了更多育儿。\n【改写】Most fathers admitted ↔ the majority of men\\.\\.\\.admitted\n多数男性即多数父亲\nthey actually did much less childcare than the mother ↔\nin reality the female actually did most of it\n女性做大部分等于父亲做更少\n【辨邻】 K K段焦点是 Half of the women said they used flexible work arrangements after\nbecoming a parent, while just 27% of men did，是另一侧面的数据，没有本句\n的\"父亲承认照护更少\"。\nmore women reported an increase in their responsibilities and manager expectations after childbirth", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "According to one father, equal parental leave is indispensable to achieving gender equality in the workplace.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】平等育儿假对职场性别平等不可或缺\n【定位】He said equal parental leave is an essential component to creating gender equality in\nthe workplace.\n他说，平等的育儿假是在职场创造性别平等的一个必要组成部分。\n【改写】equal parental leave is indispensable ↔ equal parental leave is an essential component\nindispensable即essential\nachieving gender equality in the workplace ↔ creating gender equality in the workplace\nachieving与creating同指实现平等\n【辨邻】 M M段是 culture change depends on companies putting more focus on men and\ntheir responsibilities，说话人与具体主张都不同，没有\"平等育儿假是职场平等必要条\n件\"这句话。\ncompanies need to give men paternity leave", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "One survey indicated there is now less objection to paternity leave.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】对陪产假的反对减少\n【定位】Research by the Boston College Center for Work & Family, which surveyed new parents at\nfour large US companies who were qualified for taking at least six weeks paid parental leave,\nfound that 81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed said the notion of fathers taking leave has\nbecome more acceptable.\n波士顿学院工作与家庭中心的研究调查了四家美国大公司中、有资格享受至少六周带薪育\n儿假的新晋父母，发现在1240名受访员工中，81%的人表示父亲休假的观念已变得更可\n接受。\n【改写】One survey indicated ↔\nResearch by the Boston College Center for Work & Family\\.\\.\\.found that\nsurvey即这项research\nless objection to paternity leave ↔\nthe notion of fathers taking leave has become more acceptable\n更少反对即更可接受\n【辨邻】 F F段是 these numbers were shocking to me 的主观反应，且与第40题的\"五年前对\n比\"绑定；本句无\"五年前\"也无\"惊讶\"，应落在B段的客观调查结论。\nI did not expect 80% of people to say the organisation finds dads taking this leave acceptable", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Compared to five years ago, according to one researcher, many more people said their organisation gave the same support to men and women taking parental leave.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】与五年前相比对男女支持相同\n【定位】\"We did a study on paternity leave five years ago. Compared with those findings, these\nnumbers were shocking to me. I did not expect 80% of people to say the organisation finds\ndads taking this leave acceptable and three-quarters to say it's equally supportive of women\nand men taking leave,\" Harrington said.\n\"我们五年前做过一项关于陪产假的研究。与那些发现相比，这些数字让我震惊。我没想到\n80%的人会说组织认为父亲休这种假是可接受的，四分之三的人会说它对女性和男性休假给\n予同等支持，\"Harrington说。\n【改写】Compared to five years ago, according to one researcher ↔\nWe did a study on paternity leave five years ago\n研究者即说话的Harrington\ngave the same support to men and women taking parental leave ↔\nthree-quarters to say it's equally supportive of women and men taking leave\nsame support即equally supportive\n【辨邻】 B B段是 81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed said the notion of fathers taking\nleave has become more acceptable，无时间对比，本句的\"五年前\"与\"对男女同等\n支持\"只在F段。\n81% of the 1,240 employees surveyed", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One study finds that even workers who claim to desire gender equality stick to traditional gender roles both at work and at home.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】渴望平等者仍守传统角色（职场与家庭）\n【定位】However, the study also shows how traditional gender roles endure both at work, where more\nwomen than men reported changes in their perceived career potential, and at home—even\namong workers who claim to have a strong desire for equality.\n然而，这项研究还显示，传统性别角色如何在职场（更多女性而非男性报告了对自身职业潜\n力认知的变化）和家庭中都持续存在——即便在那些声称强烈渴望平等的员工中也是如此。\n【改写】even workers who claim to desire gender equality ↔\neven among workers who claim to have a strong desire for equality\ndesire gender equality即strong desire for equality\nstick to traditional gender roles both at work and at home ↔\ntraditional gender roles endure both at work\\.\\.\\.and at home\nstick to与endure同表持续守旧\n【辨邻】 J J段是 in reality the female actually did most of it，只覆盖家庭照护，本句的\"职\n场\"与\"声称渴望平等\"两个限定它都没有，故不是源头。\nWhile about 75% of employees said both genders should give the same amount of care", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The majority of workers surveyed said parental leave policies had improved workplace culture.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】多数受访者说政策改善了职场文化\n【定位】Of those surveyed, 62% of fathers took the maximum amount of time off compared to 93% of\nmothers, and around three-quarters of workers said their employer was equally supportive of\nmothers and fathers taking parental leave and over half said leave policies had made\nworkplace culture better.\n在受访者中，62%的父亲休满了最长假期，而母亲为93%；约四分之三的员工说雇主对父母\n双方休育儿假同等支持；超过半数的人说休假政策让职场文化变得更好。\n【改写】The majority of workers surveyed said ↔ over half said\n过半即多数\nparental leave policies had improved workplace culture ↔\nleave policies had made workplace culture better\nmade better即improved", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "In spite of progress, the number of women in top positions of big companies remains extremely small.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大公司高位女性仍极少\n【定位】Despite progress, the struggle for women to reach the highest positions of power is\ndemonstrated in this year's Fortune 500 list, which featured a record 33 female CEOs, but this\nstill represents a tiny fraction of the total.\n尽管有进步，女性争取最高权力职位的艰难，在今年的财富500强榜单中可见一斑——榜单\n上有创纪录的33位女性CEO，但这在总数中仍只占极小一部分。\n【改写】In spite of progress ↔ Despite progress\nin spite of即despite\nthe number of women in top positions of big companies remains extremely small ↔\nthis still represents a tiny fraction of the total\n极小数量即极小比例", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "According to one estimate, less than one third of companies in the US provide paid parental leave.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不到三分之一公司提供带薪假\n【定位】Brad Harrington, executive director of the center and lead author of the study, estimates only\n20% to 30% of companies in the US offer paid parental leave.\n该中心执行主任、研究主笔Brad Harrington估计，美国只有20%到30%的公司提供带薪育\n儿假。\n【改写】According to one estimate ↔ estimates\nestimate动词与名词对应\nless than one third of companies in the US provide paid parental leave ↔\nonly 20% to 30% of companies in the US offer paid parental leave\n20%-30%小于三分之一\n【辨邻】 C C段是 62% of fathers took the maximum amount of time off，讲的是休假员工比\n例，与本句\"提供带薪假的公司不到三分之一\"不是同一指标。\naround three-quarters of workers said their employer was equally supportive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A number of lawsuits have pressured companies to formulate gender neutral policies on parental leave.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】多起诉讼迫使公司制定性别中立政策\n【定位】Since then, there have been a number of legal cases against companies involving paternity\nleave—including cases against JPMorgan Chase and Estee Lauder—which have helped put\npressure on companies to make their parental leave policies gender neutral.\n自那以后，出现了一系列针对公司、涉及陪产假的法律案件——包括针对摩根大通和雅诗兰\n黛的案件——它们帮助向公司施压，促其将育儿假政策改为性别中立。\n【改写】A number of lawsuits have pressured companies ↔\na number of legal cases against companies\\.\\.\\.put pressure on companies\nlawsuits即legal cases、pressured即put pressure on\nto formulate gender neutral policies on parental leave ↔\nmake their parental leave policies gender neutral\nformulate即make policies\n【辨邻】 N N段是 a historic class-action $5m settlement with JPMorgan Chase 的单一案例细\n节，没有 gender neutral 政策这一概括结果，故不是源头。\nDerek Rotondo, 35, filed the discrimination charge against his company", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "How can competitive rivalry benefit entrepreneurs according to the passage?", "options": ["A) By enabling them to outcompete other entrepreneurs.", "B) By enabling them to make their venture a success.", "C) By helping them to reach long-term goals.", "D) By helping them to stay goal-oriented."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How can competitive rivalry benefit entrepreneurs 问的是\"靠什么具体方式带来好处\"，\n答案是文中某句客观陈述，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干是 How can competitive rivalry benefit entrepreneurs，competitive rivalry（竞争\n性的对手关系）作主语，benefit 在这里是动词\"使……受益\"，entrepreneurs（创业者）是\n宾语。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Having a rival can keep you committed to achieving your goals and enhance your\noverall performance.\n有一个竞争对手能让你持续专注于实现自己的目标，并提升你的整体表现。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nBy enabling them to outcompete other entrepreneurs\"使其胜过其他创业者\"是\"拥\n有对手\"的前提情境，文章首段说的好处是专注目标、提升表现，不是\"胜过别人\"本身。\nB) 过度推断\nBy enabling them to make their venture a success\"使其事业成功\"是夸大结果；原文\n只说 enhance your overall performance\"提升表现\"，并未说能\"让事业成功\"。\nC) 无中生有\nBy helping them to reach long-term goals 多出 long-term\"长期\"这一限定；原文\nachieving your goals 只说\"目标\"，并未区分长期短期。\nD) ✓ 正确\nkeep you committed to achieving your goals ↔ helping them to stay goal-oriented\n四项中只有 helping them to stay goal-oriented 准确对应定位句\"保持专注目标\"这一\n好处，与预判一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is one of the traps entrepreneurs may often fall into when competing with rivals?", "options": ["A) They may adopt strategies that are bound to ruin their venture.", "B) They may resort to unethical means to outperform their rivals.", "C) They may be too eager to succeed while ignoring the huge labor cost.", "D) They may be intently focused on winning at the current market level."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is one of the traps 问的是文中列出的某一个具体陷阱，答案直接来自原文陈述，属\n细节题。\n【拆句】What is one of the traps entrepreneurs may often fall into when competing with\nrivals：主干问 What is one of the traps，后接 entrepreneurs…fall into 是省略了关系词\n的定语从句修饰 traps，when competing 是时间状语。fall into a trap 是固定搭配\"落入\n陷阱\"。\n【定位】They found that when people compete against their rivals, they are more willing to behave\nunethically to win.\n他们发现，当人们与对手竞争时，更愿意为了取胜而采取不道德的行为。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nstrategies that are bound to ruin their venture 用 bound to\"必定\"把后果说死；原\n文 may stain your reputation 只是\"可能\"损害声誉，并非必然毁掉事业。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbehave unethically to win ↔ resort to unethical means to outperform their rivals\nresort to unethical means to outperform their rivals 与定位句\"为赢而行不道德之\n事\"完全对应，与预判一致。\nC) 无中生有\nignoring the huge labor cost 里的 labor cost\"劳动成本\"文中从未提及；第2段讲的是\n忽视\"如何取胜\"的方式，不是忽视成本。\nD) 偷换概念\nwinning at the current market level 把第3段 next level 的\"层级\"挪来，原文从未说陷\n阱是\"只盯当前市场层级\"，属拼接错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What are entrepreneurs advised to do to avoid traps that often accompany rivalry?", "options": ["A) Deliberate on what really matters.", "B) Prioritize reaping immediate benefits.", "C) Estimate the long-term consequences.", "D) Reflect on what successes are achievable."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What are entrepreneurs advised to do 问作者建议的具体做法，答案是原文对策句的转\n述，属细节题。\n【拆句】What are entrepreneurs advised to do to avoid traps that often accompany rivalry：\n被动结构 are advised to do（被建议做某事），to avoid… 是目的状语，that often\naccompany rivalry 是定语从句修饰 traps，accompany 意为\"伴随\"。\n【定位】One way to avoid this trap is to stop and reflect on what's important.\n避免这个陷阱的一个办法是停下来，反思什么才是重要的。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nreflect on what's important ↔ Deliberate on what really matters\nDeliberate on what really matters 精确对应对策句，与预判一致。\nB) 与原文相反\nPrioritize reaping immediate benefits\"优先攫取眼前利益\"恰与原文相悖：原文 the\nloss of your integrity will have long-term consequences 是提醒别只图短期，B 却让\n人优先短期。\nC) 偷换概念\nEstimate the long-term consequences\"评估长期后果\"取自原文 long-term\nconsequences，但那是说明丧失诚信的代价，并非作者建议的做法（作者建议是\"反思\n什么重要\"）。\nD) 偷换概念\nReflect on what successes are achievable 借用了原文 reflect on，却把宾语改成\"哪\n些成功可实现\"；原文反思的对象是 what's important\"什么重要\"，而非\"成功是否\n可达\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "How can entrepreneurs avoid making impulsive and insensible decisions?", "options": ["A) By engaging themselves in critical reasoning.", "B) By developing a habit of keeping their integrity.", "C) By criticizing themselves for previous poor performances.", "D) By refraining from being too excited about their successes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How can entrepreneurs avoid making impulsive and insensible decisions 问避免冲动\n决策的具体办法，答案直取原文方法句，属细节题。\n【拆句】How can entrepreneurs avoid making impulsive and insensible decisions：avoid 后\n接动名词 making（avoid doing 固定用法），impulsive\"冲动的\"、insensible\"不明智\n的\"两个形容词并列修饰 decisions。结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】But it's possible to avoid such costly mistakes by making a habit of engaging in critical\nthinking, such as considering opposing viewpoints and conducting cost-benefit analyses,\nespecially for those decisions that are complex and can determine the future of your venture.\n但可以通过养成进行批判性思考的习惯来避免这类代价高昂的错误，比如考虑相反的观点、\n做成本收益分析，尤其是对那些复杂且能决定事业未来的决策。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nengaging in critical thinking ↔ engaging themselves in critical reasoning\nengaging themselves in critical reasoning 精确对应对策句的方法，与预判一致。\nB) 张冠李戴\nkeeping their integrity\"保持诚信\"是第2段针对\"不道德行为\"的对策，与本段\"冲动决\n策\"无关，属跨段错位。\nC) 偷换概念\ncriticizing themselves for previous poor performances\"为以往糟糕表现自责\"文中没\n有依据；原文说的是 critical thinking 批判性思考，不是\"自我批评\"。\nD) 偷换概念\nrefraining from being too excited about their successes\"别为成功太兴奋\"对错了对\n象；原文 eagerness to win 指\"求胜的急切\"导致冲动，办法是批判性思考，而非\"压抑\n对成功的兴奋\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How can entrepreneurs overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry?", "options": ["A) By paying close attention to their current performance.", "B) By taking steps that stand to endanger their rivals' success.", "C) By monitoring how their decision-making impacts their mentality.", "D) By keeping their emotions in check to avoid making poor decisions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How can entrepreneurs overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry 问克服风险效应\n的具体方法，答案直取原文，属细节题。\n【拆句】How can entrepreneurs overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry：主干 How can\nentrepreneurs overcome the effects，risk-inducing（诱发风险的）作定语修饰\neffects，of rivalry 是后置定语说明这种效应的来源。无陷阱。\n【定位】One way you can overcome the risk-inducing effects of rivalry that stand to endanger your\nventure's success is to remain attentive to your emotional state and actively monitor how such\nfeelings are affecting your decision-making.\n你能克服这种危及事业成功的、由竞争诱发风险的效应的一个办法，是持续关注自己的情绪\n状态，并主动监控这些情绪如何影响你的决策。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\npaying close attention to their current performance 把对象换成 current\nperformance\"当前表现\"；原文要关注的是 emotional state\"情绪状态\"，对象被换。\nB) 与原文相反\ntaking steps that stand to endanger their rivals' success\"采取危及对手成功的举\n措\"与原文相悖：原文 endanger your venture's success 担心的是危及\"自己\"的事业，\n办法是管情绪，不是去害对手。\nC) 与原文相反\nmonitoring how their decision-making impacts their mentality 说\"监控决策如何影\n响心态\"，方向反了；原文是 how such feelings are affecting your decision-\nmaking\"情绪如何影响决策\"，主语宾语对调。\nD) ✓ 正确\nremain attentive to your emotional state ↔ keeping their emotions in check\nhow such feelings are affecting your decision-making ↔ to avoid making poor decisions\nkeeping their emotions in check to avoid making poor decisions 整体对应对策句\n的两层意思，与预判一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does a \"multitasker\" originally refer to?", "options": ["A) An operating system capable of doing several tasks at once.", "B) A skilled worker executing more than one task at the same time.", "C) A sophisticated technology doing several tasks effectively at once.", "D) An efficient person able to perform multiple tasks at the same time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干 What does a \"multitasker\" originally refer to 直接问一个词「原本指什么」，考的\n是文中对该词来源含义的界定。\n【拆句】句子主干是 What does a \"multitasker\" originally refer to，结构平铺，refer to 意为「指\n代、指的是」，originally 作状语修饰 refer to，意为「最初指的是」。无陷阱。\n【定位】The concept does indeed come from the realms of technology, where it is used to refer to an\noperating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time.\n这个概念确实来自科技领域，在那里它被用来指一种能够同时执行多项任务的操作系统。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nan operating system that can execute multiple tasks at the same time ↔\nAn operating system capable of doing several tasks at once\n预判要的「能同时多任务的操作系统」与 An operating system capable of doing\nseveral tasks at once 完全吻合，主体、能力、同时性三点都对上。\nB) 偷换概念\nA skilled worker（熟练工人）把主体说成人，而原词最初指的是操作系统，主体张冠\n李戴。\nC) 偷换概念\nA sophisticated technology（一种复杂技术）只取了原文「来自科技领域」的背景，\n却没说成「操作系统」，主体被放大成笼统的「技术」，与原文具体所指不符。\nD) 答非所问\nAn efficient person（高效的人）把主体说成人，对应的是首句对 multitasker 的「现\n今」描述，而非题干要的「最初」所指，时间与主体都错。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why can't people really be multitaskers according to neuroscientists?", "options": ["A) They are not sufficiently exposed to multitasking situations.", "B) They are not comparable to mechanical operating systems.", "C) Their brains do not allow them to multitask.", "D) Their attention span cannot be expanded."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why can't people really be multitaskers according to neuroscientists 问的是某方\n观点下的具体理由，答案能在原文直接找到对应句。\n【拆句】Why can't people really be multitaskers according to neuroscientists：主干是 why ...\ncan't people be multitaskers（人为什么不能成为多任务者），really 加强语气，\naccording to neuroscientists 是状语，限定「按神经科学家的看法」。really 易让人误读\n为「真的能吗」，其实它服务于全句的否定，意为「人确实当不了」。\n【定位】So much so that, according to experts in neuroscience(神经系统科学), our brains do not\nhandle multitasking situations well.\n以至于，按照神经科学专家的说法，我们的大脑并不能很好地处理多任务情境。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nnot sufficiently exposed to multitasking situations（接触多任务情境不够）文中从\n未提及，原文讲的是大脑机能，不是接触多少。\nB) 答非所问\nnot comparable to mechanical operating systems（无法与机械操作系统相比）只是\n首段把人和电脑作类比的延伸，并非神经科学家给出的不能多任务的理由。\nC) ✓ 正确\nour brains do not handle multitasking situations well ↔\nTheir brains do not allow them to multitask\n预判的「大脑处理不好」与 Their brains do not allow them to multitask 一致，把原\n因归于大脑这一生理层面，正合定位句。\nD) 无中生有\nattention span cannot be expanded（注意力广度无法扩展）文中并无此说，原文只\n说两项任务争夺注意力时效率下降，没谈注意力能否扩展。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn from the conclusion of a recent study on multitasking?", "options": ["A) People make greater achievements by maintaining whatever they are doing is multitasking.", "B) People's performance benefits from the perception of what they are doing as multitasking.", "C) People's active mental processes exert a positive effect on their multitasking.", "D) People can improve their capabilities by handling multitasking situations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn from the conclusion of a recent study 问一项具体研究的结论，\n原文有对应明示句，属可直接定位的细节。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the conclusion of a recent study on multitasking：主干 what\ndo we learn（我们了解到什么），from the conclusion of a recent study 是状语，on\nmultitasking 修饰 study，指「一项关于多任务的近期研究的结论」。结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】A recent study concluded that regardless of whether people are actually handling several tasks\nor not, the mere fact that they perceive this activity as multitasking has a positive effect on\ntheir performance.\n一项近期研究得出结论：无论人们是否真的在处理多项任务，仅仅是他们把这一活动感知为\n多任务这个事实，就对他们的表现有正面影响。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nmaintaining whatever they are doing is multitasking 用 maintaining（坚持主张/坚\n持做）替换原文的 perceive（感知），「坚称是多任务」不等于「感知为多任务」，把\n主观认知偷换成口头主张。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthey perceive this activity as multitasking ↔\nthe perception of what they are doing as multitasking\nhas a positive effect on their performance ↔ performance benefits from\n预判的「感知为多任务即提升表现」与 People's performance benefits from the\nperception of what they are doing as multitasking 完全对应，落点都在「感知」这\n一主观因素。\nC) 张冠李戴\nactive mental processes（积极的心理过程）借的是第二段讲切换需要不同 mental\nprocesses 的内容，与本研究结论的「感知」无关。\nD) 与原文相反\nimprove their capabilities by handling multitasking situations 说「靠处理多任务来\n提升能力」，而原文强调的恰是「无论是否真在处理多任务」，重点在感知而非实际处\n理，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How does the business world view multitasking?", "options": ["A) It is a rare skill often found in fluid organisations.", "B) It is an adaptable capability required of all workers.", "C) It is an essential quality many employees lack.", "D) It is a desirable ability that can be developed."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How does the business world view multitasking 问商业界如何看待多任务，原文有\n专段陈述该立场，属可定位细节。\n【拆句】How does the business world view multitasking：view 在此是动词「看待」，主干为\nthe business world view multitasking（商业界如何看待多任务）。需注意 view 此处不是\n名词「景观」而是动词，属熟词的常见用法。\n【定位】From this different perspective, you can not only be multitasking but this ability can also be\ntaught: something that is easier in fluid organisations, which favour flexibility in their\nworking practices.\n从这一不同视角看，你不仅可以做到多任务，而且这种能力还可以被传授：这在灵活的组织\n里更容易做到，这类组织在工作方式上崇尚灵活性。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\na rare skill（罕见技能）与原文 many companies look for people who are skilled in\nmultitasking 矛盾——能被许多公司寻得且可传授，说明并不罕见。\nB) 以偏概全\nrequired of all workers（对所有员工的要求）把范围夸大成「全体员工」，原文只说\nmany companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking，是企业青睐某些\n人，而非要求所有人都具备。\nC) 无中生有\nmany employees lack（许多员工缺乏）文中并无此说，原文谈的是公司想要、能力可\n教，没有说员工普遍缺乏这种能力。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmany companies look for people who are skilled in multitasking ↔ a desirable ability\nthis ability can also be taught ↔ that can be developed\n预判的「可培养的实用能力」与 a desirable ability that can be developed 两点全\n中：desirable 对应被公司追求，can be developed 对应可教。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author imply we should do if we have to focus on some task and do it well?", "options": ["A) Work in a flexible way.", "B) Learn from mistakes.", "C) Avoid multitasking.", "D) Increase efficiency"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author imply we should do 用 imply 问作者「暗示」我们该怎么\n做，答案需基于原文推理而非直接摘录。\n【拆句】What does the author imply we should do if we have to focus on some task and do\nit well：主干 what does the author imply we should do（作者暗示我们该做什么），if\n引导条件状语从句 if we have to focus on some task and do it well（如果我们必须专注\n某项任务并把它做好）。从句是答案成立的前提，须连同主句一起理解。\n【定位】But having to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration\nare reduced and that can lead to more mistakes.\n但不得不同时关注好几件事，意味着专注力会下降，而这会导致更多错误。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nWork in a flexible way（以灵活方式工作）取自第四段商业视角的 favour flexibility in\ntheir working practices，与末段「要专注做好该怎么办」无关。\nB) 无中生有\nLearn from mistakes（从错误中学习）只是借了末段 mistakes 一词，原文说的是多任\n务会「导致」更多错误，从未建议「从错误中学习」。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhaving to pay attention to several things at once means that the powers of concentration are\nreduced\n↔ Avoid multitasking\n预判的「别同时做多件事」与 Avoid multitasking 一致——既然多任务降低专注、引发\n错误，那要做好就该避免它。\nD) 答非所问\nIncrease efficiency（提高效率）对应原文 Being quicker and more efficient，但那是\n多任务的「好处」一面，并非「要专注做好」的对策；况且追求效率与多任务相伴，恰\n是降低专注的根源。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
